WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.040
The grand aim of science is to figure out
how it all works, which includes giant questions

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.729
like: Where did the Universe come from?

00:00:07.729 --> 00:00:09.340
What is it really made of?

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:12.770
How does all that stuff work and interact
with each other?

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:17.640
And how is it that human intelligence exists
and is able to experience the Universe and

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.730
probe it with our minds?

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:22.460
You know, small stuff like that.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:27.000
Ever since someone stood underneath a starry
sky, looked up and went, ugh?

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.460
people have been asking these questions.

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:34.260
We can see forms of science as far back as
the ancient Mesopotamians and Egyptians, but

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:38.550
the theory of science has its roots in philosophy
with the greek philosopher Aristotle being

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:42.940
the first to think about how to actually go
about doing science.

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:48.199
This methodology has been refined over the
ages and is now called the philosophy of science.

00:00:48.199 --> 00:00:53.800
This core of science is the scientific method
which contains principles like logic, rationalism,

00:00:53.800 --> 00:01:00.379
empiricism, evidence, testable hypotheses,
objectivity, and reductionism.

00:01:00.379 --> 00:01:05.110
This is the toolkit of science and helps us
process the information we get about the universe

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:09.100
to work out what is and isn’t true.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:13.880
Mathematics and computer science are not technically
sciences because they don’t study the natural

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:18.659
world, but they are very close to science
and are incredibly useful tools for scientists

00:01:18.659 --> 00:01:20.920
to use.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:25.789
Mathematics is the machinery of physics, and
it is kind of a mystery why mathematics describes

00:01:25.789 --> 00:01:28.560
the universe so well.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:33.660
Computers are used in all branches of science
because to do science you need a brain, and

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:37.430
computers are an amazing tool to augment our
brains.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:42.570
The core of science is made of physics, chemistry
and biology, which contain a load of surrounding

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:43.850
disciplines as well.

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:48.920
I don’t have space to include them all,
but I have done in-depth maps of all of these

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:52.359
subjects so check those out if you want to
find out more.

00:01:52.359 --> 00:01:56.840
If you take a step back and look at all of
science it is interesting to see how the scientific

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:01.109
method changes as you apply it to different
subjects.

00:02:01.109 --> 00:02:05.469
Physics made huge amounts of progress to understand
the Universe through reductionism, breaking

00:02:05.469 --> 00:02:10.260
things apart into their smallest components
and understanding them one by one.

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:15.530
But when you move up the chain towards chemistry
and biology, reductionism becomes increasingly

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:21.920
difficult to apply because these subjects
deal with much more complex systems.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:26.890
If you want to study a part of biology, the
properties of a single thing are strongly

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:30.170
defined by its relationships with everything
else.

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:34.340
You can no longer study individual pieces
in isolation.

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:39.800
Because removing them from their relationships
actually changes the thing you want to study.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:46.010
So there are so many complicated relationships
in things like how your genes in your DNA

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:53.370
are expressed to make you, or how your neurons
and neurochemistry chemistry all combine to

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:57.430
create the unique experience you are having
now.

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:04.990
These systems emerge out of a massive number
of very complicated relationships and so capturing

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:09.130
all of that complexity in one model is incredibly
hard.

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:13.980
In fact as we get further and further up the
chain away from physics, the entire scientific

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:16.370
method starts breaking apart.

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:20.810
You can see this in fields like nutrition
and psychology where incredibly important

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:26.260
historical results are looking increasingly
questionable like eating saturated fat leading

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:31.450
to high cholesterol, or how it is hard to
replicate the results of many famous psychology

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:35.470
studies like willpower being a limited resource.

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:41.950
But I think that these mistakes are understandable
because trying to come up with a scientific

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:48.150
theory that explains, why people do the things
that people do is just a very hard thing to

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:52.200
do because people are… complicated!

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:58.940
And, in fact there is an argument that the
human brain is the most complex system that

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:01.060
we know about in the Universe!

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:07.700
And so when you get a load of those to together
doing stuff, like in sociology, it just gets,

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:10.980
it just gets crazy.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:15.090
Which brings us to the social sciences which
I have arranged vaguely from more sciency

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:17.040
to less sciency.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:22.250
These subjects often contain a mixture of
scientific practices and practices from the

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:27.500
humanities, like archeology which uses hard
science techniques from physics to detect

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:32.620
structures under the ground, or carbon date
objects, but then the interpretation of what

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:38.830
this evidence tells us about the humans that
lived at that time becomes a lot more subjective.

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:44.160
Or in fields like economics or political science
where people can apply rigorous statistical

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:49.930
techniques to their data, but then the interpretation
of those has to be done by a human brain who

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:52.040
is by nature subjective.

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:57.310
So the proof that the interpretations are
not scientific is the fact that they can’t

00:04:57.310 --> 00:04:58.310
predict the future.

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:03.090
So in economics it seems we can’t predict
when a recession is going to happen, or what

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:07.790
bitcoin is going to do tomorrow, whereas in
physics, if I throw a ball, I can describe

00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:11.700
the future of that ball incredibly well.

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:16.980
But please note that I’m not saying that
subjectivity is necessarily a bad thing.

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:23.020
If you’re studying a subject matter that
has these very complex system in them, and

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:27.622
you have taken the scientific method to its
breaking point, what else are you going to

00:05:27.622 --> 00:05:30.650
do, subjectivity really is all you have left.

00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:37.090
But the trouble comes when it is not recognised
or flagged appropriately, people think they

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:41.700
are being objective when they are actually
being subjective, and that does happen.

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:46.270
But this is foricing people to innovate with
the scientific method and come up with new

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:48.780
techniques to deal with these systems.

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:55.790
One example from history are randomised controlled
trials in the pharmaceutical industry to really

00:05:55.790 --> 00:06:02.360
tell if a drug is working or not and with
the recent replication crisis in the social

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:07.690
science, scientists are now trying to come
up with new techniques and methodologies to

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:09.900
deal with these complicated systems.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:14.870
And it is a very hard challenge, but that’s
how you progress.

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:19.060
But if you want to get at the truth of something
in the world, the scientific method is still

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:21.680
our best way of doing that.

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:26.280
But now lets carry on our journey and leave
the burden of scientific rigour behind us

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:31.080
and move into the arts, a place of pure subjectivity.

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:32.630
Is this good art?

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:34.840
Well I like it.

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:35.910
Okay.

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:40.090
The one thing they do share with science is
exploration, pushing the limits of what has

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:42.170
been done or thought before.

00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:47.260
They explore the human condition through books,
movies, music, art and are always trying to

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:51.400
find new ways of reflecting on what it is
to be alive, and a human with emotions and

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:54.280
a brain with the ability to create.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:58.900
And I think it is just as well we can’t
explain all of those things with science because

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:00.310
they would loose all of their charm.

00:07:00.310 --> 00:07:04.740
That intangible nature is what makes them
so much fun.

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:09.260
Some of the most enduring art has tackled
the biggest questions like what is it to be

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:10.260
a human?

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:11.260
Why are we here?

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:13.169
And what should we do with our time here?

00:07:13.169 --> 00:07:16.820
These are very similar to where we started
and brings us right back to the other side

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:18.290
of philosophy.

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:23.070
So I got a bit carried away this map isn’t
really a map of science, it is a map of human

00:07:23.070 --> 00:07:24.070
endeavour.

00:07:24.070 --> 00:07:30.490
And it isn’t even a map, but a giant cosmic
doughnut of knowledge!

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:39.700
Anyway, that’s it.

